//! trailing active byte; `0x80103500` scans those active bytes for a free
//! index; `0x801034E0` clears one; `0x80102F90`/`0x80103000` construct and
//! destroy the sub-object at `slot+0x24`; `0x80103ED0` is the teardown wrapper.
//! The subsystem is named WorldMap by the neighbouring `0x80103580` (not
//! translated), which returns 47 case labels such as `Wm_cs_mrlandsmoke`,
//! `Wm_cs_footsmoke`, `Wm_cs_landwater`, `Wm_en_burst_m` and
//! `Wm_cs_killershot`. `Wm_` is the WorldMap prefix. The active byte lives at
//! `base + (index + 1) * 0x150` - the start of the *next* record - in four
//! independent functions, so the Rust keeps the literal offset instead of
//! normalising the layout.
//!
//! # Shared transform math (cluster E)
//!
//! Eight helpers with no `Wm` string or actor field in their bodies: a
//! projection/global initialiser (`0x801025D0`), a rate conversion
//! (`0x80102E00`), an angle-based motion step (`0x80103840`), a motion reset
//! (`0x80103E20`), a weighted-vector scan (`0x80106C70`), a transform-record
//! constructor (`0x80106EE0`) and two transform setters (`0x80106F80`,
//! `0x80106FA0`). Their WorldMap membership is **not proven**, so they live
//! here under this clearly labelled section rather than in a speculative new
//! module. `0x801025D0` is the projection init: it copies `2160.0`, `-30.0`
//! and `-478.0` into `0x80321410..18`.
//!
//! # Wave 3 effect-spawn and manager notes
//!
//! `0x80103440` is the real slot allocator: its Ghidra body begins with
//! `FUN_802DCD6C()` and ends with `FUN_802DCDB8()`, but those two addresses are
//! compiler save/restore helpers (`_savegpr`/`_restgpr`), not game calls. The
//! decompiler exposes them as calls that return the incoming `(r3, r4)` pair.
//! Wave 3 therefore reconstructs the allocator from the preserved parameter
//! pair: `r3` is the effect tag and `r4` is the slot-table base. The same
//! pattern explains the leftover `undefined8` in the Ghidra listing. The
//! allocator refuses tag `0x2F`, which is the empty-slot marker written by
//! [`fn_80103150`].
//!
//! `0x80103580` returns string addresses, not Rust slices. Most of its 47
//! cases are `Wm_cs_*` world-map effects; three cases (`0x15`, `0x16`, `0x2E`)
//! are `Wm_en_*`/`Wm_mr_*` effects. The default branch returns the empty string
//! at `0x80429070` (`r13 - 0x6610`), which is also the validation string used
//! by `0x80103220`/`0x80103390`.
//!
//! `0x80103F10` tears down a global manager found through `r13 - 0x5404`
//! (`0x8042A27C`). The manager's children sit at `+0x04`, `+0x08`, `+0x0C`,
//! `+0x10`, `+0x14`, `+0x18`, `+0x1C` and `+0x20`; each is released through
//! vtable slot `+0x08` with argument `1`. The manager pointer itself is
//! cleared afterwards.
//!
//! # Small-data bases
//!
//! Both bases are set by the same `0x800041C0` block:
//!
//! * `r13 = 0x8042F680` (`addis r13,0,0x8042` / `ori r13,r13,0xF680`), shared
//!   as [`crate::engine::globals::SDA_BASE`].
//! * `r2 = 0x80433080` (`addis r2,0,0x8043` / `ori r2,r2,0x3080`, bytes at
//!   `0x8000423C`). Every r2-relative float used below was **verified from
//!   `main.elf`** at the virtual address shown next to the constant; the hex
//!   bit pattern is recorded with each constant so the value can be checked
//!   without rerunning the decompiler.
//!
//! Wave 12 adds the `r2 - 0x5708..0x5BA0` floats used by the actor effects and
//! the `r13 - 0x5404..0x5920` slots used by the scene/effect managers; each is
//! declared next to its first use in the Wave 12 section. `0x8011A680`,
//! `0x80128830` and `0x80128B00` also read the `0x8042B840` side table and the
//! `0x43300000 80000000` double constant (`2^52 + 2^31`), which is the
//! signed-integer-to-double conversion used by the PowerPC compiler.
//!
//! # Why it exists
//!
//! The world-map actor is a normal actor with a player index, a controller
//! object, a long-animation id and a small held-object pointer. The five actor
//! helpers are the smallest closed slice of that state: choose which motion
//! table applies, validate what is being carried, update the controller slot,
//! then start or stop the animation that the rest of the Wm code drives. The effect-slot cluster and the shared transform helpers join
//! them because they are the pooling and math that those actors use; keeping
//! them together lets a reader follow an effect from slot allocation to its
//! transform without opening the address-range staging lane.
//!
//! # Actor fields used here
//!
//! | offset | width | meaning | confidence |
//! |---|---|---|---|
//! | `+0x1014` | words | per-bit status array (`bit >> 5`, bit `bit & 31`) | observed |
//! | `+0x1070` | word | current long animation id (`0` = none) | observed |
//! | `+0x1090` | word | player/character variant index (`0..=3`) | inferred |
//! | `+0x14E0` | word | active controller/player slot | observed |
//! | `+0x244` | word | held-object holder; its first word is the carried actor, or `0` | inferred |
//! | `+0x153C` | byte | actor mode; `2`/`4` call `0x80147250(wm, 0)` | inferred |
//! | `+0x2A64` | word | controller/input object | observed |
//! | controller `+0x154` | word | mode id; `0x75` selects motion kind `4` | unknown |
//! | controller `+0x160` | word | controller button word | observed |
//!
//! # Dependencies
//!
//! [`crate::platform`] - big-endian volatile loads/stores and indirect calls.
//! [`crate::engine::globals::SDA_BASE`] - the verified `r13` base for the
//! small-data words used by the effect-slot and transform helpers. The
//! pick-up/release object that shares the same actor lives in
//! [`crate::game::interaction`].
//!
//! # Start here
//!
//! [`wm_motion_table`] shows how the button word becomes a motion kind, then
//! [`wm_update_held_item`] shows how the carried object is validated before
//! the action flags change. [`wm_start_anim`] / [`wm_stop_anim`] show the
//! long-animation side effects, and [`wm_set_controller_slot`] is the small
//! compare-and-set used when the active player changes. For the effect pool
//! start at [`fn_80103150`]
//! (initialise), [`fn_80103500`] (find a free slot) and [`fn_80103ed0`]
//! (teardown). The Wave 3 spawn path is
//! [`wm_effect_slot_spawn`] -> [`wm_effect_name`] -> [`wm_effect_spawn_named`];
//! [`wm_anim_frame_update`] is the best entry point for the actor animation
//! side, and [`wm_effect_manager_destroy`] shows the manager teardown order.
//! For Wave 4 start at [`wm_effect_pool_init`] (the `0x34`-byte pool), then
//! [`wm_effect_pool_find_free`] / [`wm_effect_pool_alloc_a`], and follow
//! [`wm_effect_resource_id`] -> [`wm_effect_kind`] ->
//! [`wm_effect_record_dispatch`]. For Wave 5 start at [`wm_player_init`], then
//! [`wm_player_state_ready`] -> [`wm_player_state_move`] ->
//! [`wm_player_state_approach`]; [`wm_player_sweat_effect`] is the smallest
//! example of the actor's sub-object call convention. For Wave 9 start at
//! [`wm_map_object_init`], then [`wm_map_object_refresh`] and
//! [`wm_map_object_exit_effect`]; the select/attach flow reads best as
//! [`wm_attach_event_gate`] -> [`wm_attach_begin`] -> [`wm_attach_complete`]
//! -> [`wm_attach_settle`], with [`wm_attach_range_check`] ending the attempt.
//! For Wave 27 start at [`wm_slide_begin`] -> [`wm_slide_update`] ->
//! [`wm_slide_cancel`], then [`wm_landing_state_machine`] ->
//! [`wm_landing_update`] -> [`wm_landing_recover`] for the landing side.
//! For Wave 55 start at [`fn_80108070`] (initialise the state controller),
//! then [`fn_80108260`] -> [`fn_801082d0`] for the stage transitions and
//! [`fn_80108d70`] -> [`fn_80108ea0`] for the trigger gate. For Wave 60 start
//! at [`fn_80136bb0`] (arm the slide state), then [`fn_801368f0`] (the
//! ten-case position machine), [`fn_80136650`] (halfword position record) and
//! [`fn_80135fc0`] (teardown); the approach/landing half starts at
//! [`fn_80136190`] -> [`fn_80135e70`] -> [`fn_80137130`].
//!
//! # Related code outside this lane
//!
//! Both source lanes (`r80100_80140` and `r80140_80180`) are fully promoted.
//! These callees still live outside them and are called by absolute address:
//!
//! * `0x801299B0` - final call of [`wm_update_held_item`]; role unknown.
//! * `0x80060720` / `0x80060750` - shared long-animation audio helpers, still
//!   called by absolute address because they live outside this lane.
//!
//! `0x80147250` used to be in this list; wave 3 translated it as
//! [`wm_set_mode_byte`]. The earlier [`wm_update_held_item`] still calls the
//! raw address through `SET_MODE` so its original indirect call is unchanged.
//!
//! # Glossary
//!
//! * **actor** - a game object. Its first word is a vtable pointer; `+0x60` is
//!   the virtual base used by the actor system.
//! * **world map (Wm)** - the overworld/level-select map.
//! * **motion table** - an array of animation/motion descriptors selected by
//!   player index and button state.
//! * **DOL** - the Wii executable container (`sys/main.dol`).
//!
//! # Confidence summary
//!
//! [`wm_motion_table`] and [`wm_set_controller_slot`] are `high`: every branch
//! and store is visible. [`wm_start_anim`], [`wm_stop_anim`] and
//! [`wm_update_held_item`] are `medium` because the animation/controller and
//! held-object field roles are inferred from their call sites; the calls and
//! stores themselves are exact. Of the promoted
//! `r80100_80140` helpers the slot-table, motion and transform-setter bodies
//! are `high`; [`fn_80103ed0`] and [`fn_80106c70`] are `medium` because a
//! callee argument or the `-1.0` result is inferred.
//!
//! Wave 3: [`wm_effect_name`], [`wm_effect_slot_alloc`],
//! [`wm_effect_slot_spawn`], [`wm_effect_manager_destroy`],
//! [`wm_anim_table_entry`], [`wm_set_mode_byte`] and the small setters are
//! `high`; the Ghidra body and all stores are explicit. [`wm_effect_motion_step`],
//! [`wm_effect_weight`], [`wm_effect_spawn_named`], [`wm_anim_frame_update`],
//! [`wm_set_sound_by_mode`], [`wm_update_target_state`] and
//! [`wm_reset_facing_and_notify`] are `medium` because Ghidra drops at least one
//! argument or the field roles are inferred from neighbouring functions.
//!
//! Wave 55: the literal setters, the two empty hooks and the single-global
//! clear are `high`; the bodies whose virtual receiver, `r13` object role or
//! callee identity is inferred are `medium` (fourteen of the thirty).
use crate::engine::globals::SDA_BASE;
use crate::game::bases::{interface_call2, interface_call3};
use crate::platform;
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Actor field offsets and table addresses
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Base of the actor's per-bit status words in the original object.
///
/// `0x80056CF0` tests one bit of this array; every caller indexes
/// `+0x1014 + (bit >> 5) * 4`. `// observed:` the helper body in
/// `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80056cf0_FUN_80056cf0.c`.
const STATUS_WORDS: usize = 0x1014;
/// `0x80142510`: animation-table selector. Returns
/// `*(0x8032528C + index * 4) + kind * 0x40`. `// observed:` whole body of
/// `80142510_FUN_80142510.c`.
const ANIM_TABLE_SELECT: usize = 0x8014_2510;
/// Fallback table returned when action flags `0x3C`/`0x3E` put the actor in
/// the "special" mode. `// observed:` `&DAT_802f5500`.
const ANIM_TABLE_SPECIAL: usize = 0x802F_5500;
/// Fallback table returned while action flag `0x3B` is set. `// observed:`
/// `&DAT_802f5540`.
const ANIM_TABLE_FLAG_3B: usize = 0x802F_5540;
/// Actor field `+0x2A64`: pointer to the controller/input object used by the
/// animation and camera helpers.
const CONTROLLER: usize = 0x2A64;
/// Actor field `+0x1090`: player/character variant index (`0..=3`).
const PLAYER_INDEX: usize = 0x1090;
/// Actor field `+0x1070`: current long animation id (`0` = none).
const ANIM_ID: usize = 0x1070;
/// Actor field `+0x244`: pointer to the held-object holder. Its first word is
/// the carried actor, or `0`. `// inferred:` the two-level shape and the
/// "held item" name come from [`wm_update_held_item`]'s checks.
const HELD_HOLDER: usize = 0x244;
/// Actor field `+0x153C`: mode byte. Modes `2` and `4` force the
/// `0x80147250` call at the end of [`wm_update_held_item`].
const MODE_BYTE: usize = 0x153C;
/// `0x80147250`: writes the actor mode byte `+0x153C`, forcing `1` when the
/// actor's vtable slot `+0xE0` vetoes it. Wave 3 translates the body as
/// [`wm_set_mode_byte`]; [`wm_update_held_item`] keeps the raw address so its
/// original indirect call remains visible.
const SET_MODE: usize = 0x8014_7250;
/// `0x801299B0`: final call of [`wm_update_held_item`]. Role unknown; outside
/// this lane and not translated.
const FUN_801299B0: usize = 0x8012_99B0;
/// `0x80060720`: start the shared long-animation audio/stream state. Its body
/// reads `r13-0x599C`, so it is not translated in this lane.
const AUDIO_LONG_ANIM_START: usize = 0x8006_0720;
/// `0x80060750`: stop the shared long-animation audio/stream state when no
/// player still runs an animation above `0x3B`. Also `r13`-based.
const AUDIO_LONG_ANIM_STOP: usize = 0x8006_0750;
/// Tests status bit `bit` (0..=255) of `actor`. Mirrors `0x80056CF0`.
unsafe fn flag_test(actor: usize, bit: u32) -> bool {
    let offset = STATUS_WORDS + ((bit >> 5) as usize) * 4;
    let word = platform::read_u32(actor + offset);
    (word >> (bit & 31)) & 1 != 0
}

/// Sets status bit `bit` (0..=255) of `actor`. Mirrors `0x80056C70`.
unsafe fn flag_set(actor: usize, bit: u32) {
    let offset = STATUS_WORDS + ((bit >> 5) as usize) * 4;
    let word = platform::read_u32(actor + offset);
    platform::write_u32(actor + offset, word | (1u32 << (bit & 31)));
}

/// Clears status bit `bit` (0..=255) of `actor`. Mirrors `0x80056CB0`.
unsafe fn flag_clear(actor: usize, bit: u32) {
    let offset = STATUS_WORDS + ((bit >> 5) as usize) * 4;
    let word = platform::read_u32(actor + offset);
    platform::write_u32(actor + offset, word & !(1u32 << (bit & 31)));
}

/// Calls virtual slot `slot` (a byte offset) of `object`, whose vtable pointer
/// is its first word.
#[inline]
unsafe fn vcall0(object: usize, slot: usize) -> u32 {
    let vtable = platform::read_u32(object) as usize;
    let entry = platform::read_u32(vtable + slot) as usize;
    platform::call1(entry, object as u32)
}

// ===========================================================================
// Motion table and controller slot
// ===========================================================================
/// Picks the motion/animation table for the actor's current input state.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80142580` at `0x80142580` (220 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80142580_FUN_80142580.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `wm` - world-map actor.
///
/// # Returns
/// Pointer to the selected animation table entry:
/// * `0x802F5500` when status flag `0x3C` is clear or flag `0x3E` is set;
/// * `0x802F5540` when flag `0x3B` is set;
/// * otherwise `0x80142510(player_index, kind)` with `kind` decoded from the
///   controller button word at `controller+0x160`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// None - pure selection.
///
/// # Safety
/// `wm` must be live and `+0x2A64` must point at a readable controller.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the table arithmetic is fully visible in `0x80142510`; only the
/// button-bit meanings (`1/4/0x10`, and `+0x154 == 0x75`) are inferred.
pub unsafe fn wm_motion_table(wm: usize) -> usize {
    if !flag_test(wm, 0x3C) || flag_test(wm, 0x3E) {
        if !flag_test(wm, 0x3B) {
            let controller = platform::read_u32(wm + CONTROLLER) as usize;
            let buttons = platform::read_u32(controller + 0x160);
            // inferred: decode the button word into a 0..=4 motion kind. The
            // priority order is exactly the original's nested if/else chain.
            let kind = if buttons & 1 == 0 {
                if buttons & 4 == 0 {
                    if buttons & 0x10 == 0 {
                        // unknown: controller+0x154 == 0x75 selects kind 4;
                        // the field looks like a mode id but is not proven.
                        if platform::read_u32(controller + 0x154) == 0x75 {
                            4
                        } else {
                            0
                        }
                    } else {
                        3
                    }
                } else {
                    2
                }
            } else if platform::read_u32(wm + PLAYER_INDEX) != 3 {
                1
            } else {
                0
            };
            platform::call3(
                ANIM_TABLE_SELECT,
                wm as u32,
                platform::read_u32(wm + PLAYER_INDEX),
                kind,
            ) as usize
        } else {
            ANIM_TABLE_FLAG_3B
        }
    } else {
        ANIM_TABLE_SPECIAL
    }
}

/// Sets the active controller/player slot.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80145ac0` at `0x80145ac0` (140 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80145ac0_FUN_80145ac0.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `wm` - world-map actor.
/// * `slot` - new controller/player slot value.
///
/// # Returns
/// `0` when flags `4` or `6` block the change, or when the value is already
/// set; otherwise `1` when `slot` differs from the actor's own index
/// (`+0x1090`), else `0`.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Writes `slot` to `+0x14E0`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `wm` must be live.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the compare-and-set and the sign-bit result are explicit.
pub unsafe fn wm_set_controller_slot(wm: usize, slot: u32) -> u32 {
    if flag_test(wm, 4) || flag_test(wm, 6) {
        return 0;
    }
    if platform::read_u32(wm + 0x14E0) == slot {
        return 0;
    }
    platform::write_u32(wm + 0x14E0, slot);
    let own = platform::read_u32(wm + PLAYER_INDEX);
    // observed: `(slot - own | own - slot) >> 31` = "slot != own".
    (slot.wrapping_sub(own) | own.wrapping_sub(slot)) >> 31
}

// ===========================================================================
// Long animation start/stop
// ===========================================================================
/// Starts a long animation on the actor.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80145bd0` at `0x80145bd0` (128 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80145bd0_FUN_80145bd0.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `wm` - world-map actor.
/// * `immediate` - when `0`, first plays actor effect `0x37`.
/// * `anim_id` - animation id; `0` leaves the actor untouched.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// * May call the actor's vtable slot `+0x434` with `(0x37, 0)`.
/// * Stores `anim_id` at `+0x1070`.
/// * For ids above `0x3B`, starts the shared audio state.
/// * Calls the controller object's vtable slot `+0x40`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `wm` must be live; `+0x2A64` must point at a valid controller object.
///
/// # Confidence
/// medium - field roles are inferred; the calls and stores are exact.
pub unsafe fn wm_start_anim(wm: usize, immediate: u32, anim_id: u32) {
    if immediate == 0 {
        // observed: effect id 0x37, second argument 0. The actor virtual base
        // is a member at +0x60, so `this` stays the actor pointer.
        let vtable = platform::read_u32(wm + 0x60) as usize;
        let entry = platform::read_u32(vtable + 0x434) as usize;
        let _ = platform::call3(entry, wm as u32, 0x37, 0);
    }
    if anim_id != 0 {
        platform::write_u32(wm + ANIM_ID, anim_id);
        if anim_id > 0x3B {
            platform::call0(AUDIO_LONG_ANIM_START);
        }
        let controller = platform::read_u32(wm + CONTROLLER) as usize;
        let _ = vcall0(controller, 0x40);
    }
}

/// Stops the actor's long animation and releases the shared audio state.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80145c50` at `0x80145c50` (104 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80145c50_FUN_80145c50.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `wm` - world-map actor.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// When `+0x1070` is non-zero: clears it, calls controller vtable slots
/// `+0x44` and `+0x4C`, then calls the shared audio stop helper.
///
/// # Safety
/// `wm` must be live; `+0x2A64` must point at a valid controller object.
///
/// # Confidence
/// medium - same inference level as [`wm_start_anim`].
pub unsafe fn wm_stop_anim(wm: usize) {
    if platform::read_u32(wm + ANIM_ID) != 0 {
        platform::write_u32(wm + ANIM_ID, 0);
        let controller = platform::read_u32(wm + CONTROLLER) as usize;
        let _ = vcall0(controller, 0x44);
        let _ = vcall0(controller, 0x4C);
        platform::call0(AUDIO_LONG_ANIM_STOP);
    }
}

// ===========================================================================
// Held-item state
//// The world-map actor can carry another actor. `+0x244` holds a holder whose
// first word is the carried actor; this helper clears the "action" flags and
// re-derives whether the carried object still counts as active.
// ===========================================================================
/// Updates the object the actor is holding.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80144030` at `0x80144030` (204 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80144030_FUN_80144030.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// * `wm` - world-map actor.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// * Clears status flags `0xA7`, `0x0E`, `0x4D`.
/// * Sets flag `0xB7` when the held object is not an active carried item.
/// * In mode `2` or `4` (`+0x153C`), calls `0x80147250(wm, 0)`.
/// * Calls `0x801299B0(wm)` at the end.
///
/// # Safety
/// `wm` must be live; `+0x244` must be `0` or point at a holder whose first
/// word is `0` or a live actor.
///
/// # Confidence
/// medium - the flag writes are exact; the "held item" semantics of `+0x244`
/// and the `0x97` type id are inferred from the shape. The function stayed in
/// the staging lane until the final pass, when it was accepted as the only
/// inferred-role member of the world-map actor group.
pub unsafe fn wm_update_held_item(wm: usize) {
    flag_clear(wm, 0xA7);
    flag_clear(wm, 0x0E);
    flag_clear(wm, 0x4D);
    let mut active = false;
    let holder = platform::read_u32(wm + HELD_HOLDER) as usize;
    if holder != 0 {
        let actor = platform::read_u32(holder) as usize;
        if actor != 0 {
            // observed: type id 0x97 at +8, and bits 0x10/0x20 at +0x120,
            // mark the held object as active.
            active = platform::read_u16(actor + 0x8) == 0x97;
            let flags = platform::read_u32(actor + 0x120);
            if flags & 0x10 != 0 || flags & 0x20 != 0 {
                active = true;
            }
        }
    }
    if !active {
        flag_set(wm, 0xB7);
    }
    let mode = platform::read_u8(wm + MODE_BYTE);
    if mode == 4 || mode == 2 {
        platform::call2(SET_MODE, wm as u32, 0);
    }
    // unknown: 0x801299B0 is outside this lane and not translated here.
    platform::call1(FUN_801299B0, wm as u32);
}

// ===========================================================================
// Promoted from r80100_80140 (clusters D-E)
//// These fifteen functions were moved verbatim from
// `src/subsystems/r80100_80140.rs` in the lane's second promotion pass; that
// staging file no longer contains code. The ledger rows now record
// `module=game` and `rust_file=src/game/worldmap.rs`. Address order is
// preserved inside each section.
// ===========================================================================
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Shared small-data addresses and r2 float constants
//// The r2 base is set by the same `0x800041C0` block that sets r13:
// `addis r2,0,0x8043` / `ori r2,r2,0x3080` (bytes `3C 40 80 43` 
/// `60 42 30 80` at `0x8000423C`), so r2 = 0x80433080.
//// Every float below was verified from `main.elf` at the virtual address shown;
// the hex bit pattern is recorded so a reader can cross-check the value without
// rerunning the decompiler.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Secondary small-data base register r2 for main-`DOL` floating constants.
///
/// Observed: `0x800041C0` contains `3C 40 80 43 60 42 30 80` at
/// `0x8000423C` = `addis r2,0,0x8043` / `ori r2,r2,0x3080`.
const SDA2_BASE: usize = 0x8043_3080;
/// `r13 - 0x54C0` (absolute `0x8042A1C0`): word written with the r2 constant
/// 38 by [`fn_801025d0`].
const SDA_54C0_WORD: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x54C0;
/// `r13 - 0x53F0` (absolute `0x8042A290`): optional pointer consumed by
/// [`fn_80106ee0`]. Role unknown.
const SDA_53F0_PTR: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x53F0;
/// `r13 - 0x7D52` (absolute `0x8042792E`): 16-bit value read by the external
/// getter `0x8006A230`, inlined in [`fn_80102e00`].
const SDA_7D52_U16: usize = SDA_BASE - 0x7D52;
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// r2-relative floating constants
//// Every value below was read from main.elf at the virtual address shown. The
// r2 base is verified above; the float bit patterns are included so a reader
// can cross-check the decompiler without rerunning it.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// `r2 - 0x5F3C` = `0x8042D144`: `2160.0f` (`0x45070000`).
const R2_5F3C_2160: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5F3C;
/// `r2 - 0x5F38` = `0x8042D148`: `-30.0f` (`0xC1F00000`).
const R2_5F38_NEG30: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5F38;
/// `r2 - 0x5F34` = `0x8042D14C`: `-478.0f` (`0xC3EF0000`).
const R2_5F34_NEG478: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5F34;
/// `r2 - 0x6114` = `0x8042CF6C`: integer `23` (`0x00000017`).
const R2_6114_23: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x6114;
/// `r2 - 0x60FC` = `0x8042CF84`: integer `38` (`0x00000026`).
const R2_60FC_38: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x60FC;
/// `r2 - 0x5F18` = `0x8042D168`: `3600.0f` (`0x45610000`).
const R2_5F18_3600: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5F18;
/// `r2 - 0x5EE0` = `0x8042D1A0`: `360.0f` (`0x43B40000`).
const R2_5EE0_360: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5EE0;
/// `r2 - 0x5EDC` = `0x8042D1A4`: `0.7111111f` (`0x3F360B61`), the float
/// nearest to `360/506.25`; the role of the ratio is unknown.
const R2_5EDC_0_7111111: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5EDC;
/// `r2 - 0x5EB8` = `0x8042D1C8`: `0.0f` (`0x00000000`).
const R2_5EB8_ZERO: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5EB8;
/// `r2 - 0x5EA4` = `0x8042D1DC`: `9999.0f` (`0x461C3C00`).
const R2_5EA4_9999: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5EA4;
/// `r2 - 0x5E78` = `0x8042D208`: `0.0f` (`0x00000000`).
const R2_5E78_ZERO: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5E78;
/// `r2 - 0x5E74` = `0x8042D20C`: `-1.0f` (`0xBF800000`).
const R2_5E74_NEG1: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5E74;
/// `r2 - 0x5E38` = `0x8042D248`: `0.0f` (`0x00000000`).
const R2_5E38_ZERO: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5E38;
/// `r2 - 0x5E34` = `0x8042D24C`: `8.0f` (`0x41000000`).
const R2_5E34_EIGHT: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5E34;
/// `r2 - 0x5E30` = `0x8042D250`: `16.0f` (`0x41800000`).
const R2_5E30_SIXTEEN: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5E30;
/// `r2 - 0x5E2C` = `0x8042D254`: `-16.0f` (`0xC1800000`).
const R2_5E2C_NEG16: usize = SDA2_BASE - 0x5E2C;
/// `0x80329958`: address stored as the vtable word at `slot + 0x24` by the
/// constructor [`fn_80102f90`] and restored by the destructor
/// [`fn_80103000`].
const VTABLE_80329958: usize = 0x8032_9958;
/// `0x80377DC8`, `0x80377DCC`, `0x80377DD0`: three 32-bit initial values copied
/// into each slot by [`fn_80103150`]. Their meaning is unknown.
const INIT_WORD_80377DC8: usize = 0x8037_7DC8;
const INIT_WORD_80377DCC: usize = 0x8037_7DCC;
const INIT_WORD_80377DD0: usize = 0x8037_7DD0;
/// `0x80321404`, `0x80321410`, `0x80321414`, `0x80321418`: four words
/// initialised by [`fn_801025d0`] from r2 constants. Their consumers are not
/// translated; `0x80321410..18` look like three floats (2160, -30, -478).
const G_80321404: usize = 0x8032_1404;
const G_80321410: usize = 0x8032_1410;
const G_80321414: usize = 0x8032_1414;
const G_80321418: usize = 0x8032_1418;
/// `0x80102640`: code address passed as the second argument of
/// `0x802DC780` by [`fn_801025d0`]. Role unknown.
const CODE_80102640: usize = 0x8010_2640;
/// `0x80373D70`: data address passed to `0x802DC780` by [`fn_801025d0`].
const DATA_80373D70: usize = 0x8037_3D70;
/// `0x80321BD8`: vtable/type word stored at `object + 0x18` by
/// [`fn_80106ee0`]. Role unknown beyond the store.
const VTABLE_80321BD8: usize = 0x8032_1BD8;
/// Slot table geometry observed in `0x80102F90..0x80103ED0`.
///
/// - each record reserves 0x150 bytes;
/// - the record count is 0x55;
/// - the "active" byte tested by [`fn_80103500`] sits at record base + 0x150,
///   i.e. at the start of the *next* record. This is preserved literally.
const SLOT_STRIDE: usize = 0x150;
const SLOT_COUNT: i32 = 0x55;
const SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET: usize = 0x150;
/// Offset of the sub-object inside a slot record (`slot + 0x24`).
const SLOT_SUBOBJECT_OFFSET: usize = 0x24;
/// Initial value written to `slot + 0x04` by [`fn_80103150`].
/// `// unknown:` likely a state/type tag; no consumer was translated.
const SLOT_INITIAL_TAG: u32 = 0x2F;
/// Active-byte offsets for the five slots visited by one unrolled scan group.
/// Slot *n* uses `SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET + n * SLOT_STRIDE`.
const SLOT_ACTIVE_1: usize = SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET + SLOT_STRIDE; // 0x2A0
const SLOT_ACTIVE_2: usize = SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET + 2 * SLOT_STRIDE; // 0x3F0
const SLOT_ACTIVE_3: usize = SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET + 3 * SLOT_STRIDE; // 0x540
const SLOT_ACTIVE_4: usize = SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET + 4 * SLOT_STRIDE; // 0x690
/// `0x8016D4B0`, `0x802B9280`, `0x802D7AA0`, `0x802D7B20`: callees outside
/// this lane. Names and full behaviour are unknown; each is called through
/// [`crate::platform`] with the argument count shown by Ghidra.
const FUN_8016D4B0: usize = 0x8016_D4B0;
const FUN_8022D2F0: usize = 0x8022_D2F0;
const FUN_8022DDE0: usize = 0x8022_DDE0;
const FUN_80237BD0: usize = 0x8023_7BD0;
const FUN_802B9280: usize = 0x802B_9280;
const FUN_802D7AA0: usize = 0x802D_7AA0;
const FUN_802D7B20: usize = 0x802D_7B20;
const FUN_802DC780: usize = 0x802D_C780;
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Local float and signed-integer helpers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
/// Reads a big-endian IEEE-754 single-precision value.
///
/// `platform` exposes only integer reads, so the bit pattern is assembled with
/// `read_u32` and reinterpreted without any host-endianness assumption.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr..addr+4` must be readable.
unsafe fn read_f32(addr: usize) -> f32 {
    f32::from_bits(platform::read_u32(addr))
}

/// Writes a big-endian IEEE-754 single-precision value.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr..addr+4` must be writable.
unsafe fn write_f32(addr: usize, value: f32) {
    platform::write_u32(addr, value.to_bits());
}

/// Reads a big-endian signed 16-bit value.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr..addr+2` must be readable.
unsafe fn read_i16(addr: usize) -> i16 {
    platform::read_u16(addr) as i16
}

/// Writes a big-endian signed 16-bit value.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr..addr+2` must be writable.
unsafe fn write_i16(addr: usize, value: i16) {
    platform::write_u16(addr, value as u16);
}

/// Calls a PowerPC function `f64 -> f64`.
///
/// Used for `0x80237BD0`, whose Ghidra prototype returns `double`. On the
/// 32-bit PowerPC EABI a double result arrives in `f1`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr` must have the matching ABI and return a double.
unsafe fn call_f64_f64(addr: usize, a: f64) -> f64 {
    let f = crate::platform::guest_fn!(addr, fn(f64) -> f64);
    f(a)
}

/// Calls a PowerPC function `(f64, f64, u32) -> ()`.
///
/// Used for `0x8022D2F0`, whose third argument is an integer pointer and whose
/// first two arguments are doubles.
///
/// # Safety
/// `addr` must have the matching ABI and accept the pointer value in `r3`.
unsafe fn call_f64_f64_u32(addr: usize, a: f64, b: f64, c: u32) {
    let f = crate::platform::guest_fn!(addr, fn(f64, f64, u32));
    f(a, b, c)
}

// ===========================================================================
// WorldMap effect slot table (cluster D)
//// The 0x55-record slot pool used by the WorldMap effect subsystem. Evidence:
// the neighbouring 0x80103580 - now [`wm_effect_name`] - returns strings such
// as `Wm_cs_mrlandsmoke`, `Wm_cs_footsmoke`, `Wm_cs_landwater` and
// `Wm_en_burst_m`. The two cluster-E motion helpers 0x80103840 and 0x80103E20
// are numerically interleaved with these addresses but live in the shared
// transform section below.
// ===========================================================================
/// Constructs the sub-object at `base + 0x24`.
///
/// Calls `0x802D7AA0(base + 0x24)`, overwrites the first word with the vtable
/// address `0x80329958`, then clears the fields at `+0x138`, `+0x13C`,
/// `+0x140`, `+0x141`, `+0x144` and `+0x148`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80102F90` at `0x80102F90` (104 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80102f90_FUN_80102f90.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `base`: address of a 0x150-byte slot. Its sub-object starts at `+0x24`.
///
/// # Returns
/// `base`, unchanged (constructor convention).
///
/// # Side effects
/// Calls `0x802D7AA0` and writes the vtable plus six fields inside the slot.
///
/// # Safety
/// `base` must point to a writable 0x14C-byte region; `0x802D7AA0` must accept
/// the sub-object pointer.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high for the writes; medium for the "constructor" role.
pub unsafe fn fn_80102f90(base: usize) -> usize {
    let sub = base + SLOT_SUBOBJECT_OFFSET;
    platform::call1(FUN_802D7AA0, sub as u32);
    platform::write_u32(sub, VTABLE_80329958 as u32);
    platform::write_u32(base + 0x138, 0);
    platform::write_u32(base + 0x13C, 0);
    platform::write_u8(base + 0x140, 0);
    platform::write_u8(base + 0x141, 0);
    platform::write_u32(base + 0x144, 0);
    platform::write_u32(base + 0x148, 0);
    base
}

/// Destroys the sub-object at `base + 0x24` and optionally frees `base`.
///
/// Restores the vtable, calls `0x8016D4B0(sub)`, then calls
/// `0x802D7B20(sub, 0)`. When `free_base > 0`, calls `0x802B9280(base)`
/// afterwards. Returns `base` even when it is null.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80103000` at `0x80103000` (136 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80103000_FUN_80103000.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `base`: slot address, or 0.
/// - `free_base`: when positive, run the final free call. Ghidra's `int`
///   comparison is preserved (`> 0`).
///
/// # Returns
/// `base` (null is returned unchanged).
///
/// # Side effects
/// Writes the vtable at `base + 0x24`, calls two destructor helpers, and
/// possibly `0x802B9280(base)`.
///
/// # Safety
/// When `base != 0`, `base + 0x24` must be a valid sub-object. All three call
/// targets must accept the arguments shown.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the branch structure and call sequence are explicit.
pub unsafe fn fn_80103000(base: usize, free_base: i32) -> usize {
    if base != 0 {
        let sub = base + SLOT_SUBOBJECT_OFFSET;
        // observed: this check is redundant because base != 0 implies
        // base + 0x24 != 0 on the 32-bit target; kept for fidelity.
        if sub != 0 {
            platform::write_u32(sub, VTABLE_80329958 as u32);
            platform::call1(FUN_8016D4B0, sub as u32);
            platform::call2(FUN_802D7B20, sub as u32, 0);
        }
        if free_base > 0 {
            platform::call1(FUN_802B9280, base as u32);
        }
    }
    base
}

/// Initialises all 0x55 slot records and their trailing active bytes.
///
/// For each record the function writes `+0x04 = 0x2F`, clears
/// `+0x08..+0x18`, copies three globals into `+0x1C..+0x24`, advances by
/// 0x150 and clears the byte at the new base. Consequently the last clear lands
/// at `base + 0x55 * 0x150`, i.e. one byte past the 0x55 records.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80103150` at `0x80103150` (84 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80103150_FUN_80103150.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `base`: address of the first 0x150-byte record. Must be writable for
///   `0x55 * 0x150 + 1` bytes.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Writes 0x55 records and the trailing active byte. Reads
/// `0x80377DC8`, `0x80377DCC` and `0x80377DD0` once per record.
///
/// # Safety
/// The whole `0x55 * 0x150 + 1` byte region must be writable and must not
/// overlap the three global initial-value words.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the loop body is fully explicit. The record layout is unusual; it is
/// preserved literally rather than normalised.
pub unsafe fn fn_80103150(base: usize) {
    let mut record = base;
    let mut remaining: i32 = SLOT_COUNT;
    loop {
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x04, SLOT_INITIAL_TAG);
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x08, 0);
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x0C, 0);
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x10, 0);
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x14, 0);
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x18, 0);
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x1C, platform::read_u32(INIT_WORD_80377DC8));
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x20, platform::read_u32(INIT_WORD_80377DCC));
        platform::write_u32(record + 0x24, platform::read_u32(INIT_WORD_80377DD0));
        record += SLOT_STRIDE;
        platform::write_u8(record, 0);
        remaining -= 1;
        if remaining == 0 {
            return;
        }
    }
}

/// Clears the active byte associated with slot `index`.
///
/// The byte is at `base + index * 0x150 + 0x150`. This is literally the first
/// byte of the following record, matching the layout used by
/// [`fn_80103500`] and the allocator at `0x80103440`.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_801034E0` at `0x801034E0` (20 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/801034e0_FUN_801034e0.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `base`: address of slot record 0.
/// - `index`: signed slot index; no bounds check.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Writes one zero byte.
///
/// # Safety
/// The computed byte address must be writable.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - single store with an explicit offset.
pub unsafe fn fn_801034e0(base: usize, index: i32) {
    let addr = (base as isize
        + (index as isize) * SLOT_STRIDE as isize
        + SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET as isize) as usize;
    platform::write_u8(addr, 0);
}

/// Finds the first slot whose active byte is zero.
///
/// Scans 0x55 slots in groups of five. The active byte for slot *i* is at
/// `base + (i + 1) * 0x150`, i.e. at the start of the next record. Returns the
/// slot index, or -1 when all 0x55 slots are active.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80103500` at `0x80103500` (128 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80103500_FUN_80103500.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `base`: address of slot record 0.
///
/// # Returns
/// First free index in `0..0x55`, or `-1` if none is free.
///
/// # Side effects
/// None; only reads active bytes.
///
/// # Safety
/// The active bytes `base + 0x150 + i * 0x150` for `i in 0..0x55` must be
/// readable. That is 0x55 bytes spread over the table.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the unrolled five-way test and the 0x11 iteration count are explicit.
pub unsafe fn fn_80103500(base: usize) -> i32 {
    let mut record = base;
    let mut index: i32 = 0;
    let mut groups: i32 = 0x11;
    loop {
        // `found` mirrors Ghidra's `iVar2`, which is set before each test.
        let mut found = index;
        let mut hit = false;
        if platform::read_u8(record + SLOT_ACTIVE_OFFSET) == 0 {
            hit = true;
        } else {
            found = index + 1;
            if platform::read_u8(record + SLOT_ACTIVE_1) == 0 {
                hit = true;
            } else {
                found = index + 2;
                if platform::read_u8(record + SLOT_ACTIVE_2) == 0 {
                    hit = true;
                } else {
                    found = index + 3;
                    if platform::read_u8(record + SLOT_ACTIVE_3) == 0 {
                        hit = true;
                    } else {
                        found = index + 4;
                        if platform::read_u8(record + SLOT_ACTIVE_4) == 0 {
                            hit = true;
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
        if hit {
            return found;
        }
        record += 0x690;
        index += 5;
        groups -= 1;
        if groups == 0 {
            break;
        }
    }
    if index == SLOT_COUNT {
        -1
    } else {
        index
    }
}

/// Copies three consecutive 32-bit words from `src` into `dst + 0x1C`.
///
/// The source reads happen in the order `src[1]`, `src[2]`, `src[0]`, matching
/// the Ghidra temporary order. This matters if source and destination overlap.
///
/// # Original
/// - Ghidra: `FUN_80103D90` at `0x80103D90` (28 bytes)
/// - Evidence: `work/ghidra/main-gb/export/80103d90_FUN_80103d90.c`
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `dst`: destination object base.
/// - `src`: source vector of three 32-bit words.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Writes `dst + 0x1C`, `+0x20` and `+0x24`.
///
/// # Safety
/// `src..src+12` must be readable and the three destination words writable.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - three loads and three stores, order preserved.
pub unsafe fn fn_80103d90(dst: usize, src: usize) {
    let y = platform::read_u32(src + 4);
    let z = platform::read_u32(src + 8);
    let x = platform::read_u32(src);
    platform::write_u32(dst + 0x1C, x);
    platform::write_u32(dst + 0x20, y);
    platform::write_u32(dst + 0x24, z);
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// 0x80103DB0 - install a transform record by index, or the last record
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// Copies transform record `index` into `this + 0x1C`, or the last record for a
/// negative index.
///
/// For a non-negative `index` the source is the record table word at
/// `*(this + 4)`, indexed directly. A negative `index` selects
/// `count - 1`, where `count` is the word at `table + 8` (the count field that
/// [`wm_transform_record_copy`] skips over). Both arms then call
/// [`fn_80103d90`] to store the three words at `this + 0x1C..0x24`.
///
/// # Original (untabled)
/// - Ghidra: none - no `functions.tsv` row and no export file for `0x80103DB0`.
/// - Extent: `0x80103DB0..0x80103E20` (112 bytes, 28 words), ending at the
///   `blr` at `0x80103E1C`; it sits between [`fn_80103d90`] (the three-word
///   store) and `0x80103E20`; [`fn_80103ed0`] follows the same band.
/// - Reach: no `bl` in the main image; one `b` in `d_en_bossNP` at
///   `0x80C51300`. No data word names it.
/// - Observed: `stwu r1,-0x30(r1)`; `mflr r0`; `cmpi cr0,0,r4,0`;
///   `mr r5,r4`; `stw r0,0x34(r1)`; `stw r31,0x2C(r1)`; `mr r31,r3`;
///   `40800028 bc 4,0` (`bf cr0.lt`, to the non-negative arm at
///   `0x80103DF4`); negative arm: `lwz r4,4(r31)`; `addi r3,r1,0x14`;
///   `lwz r5,8(r4)`; `addi r5,r5,-1`; `bl 0x80106B10`; `mr r3,r31`;
///   `addi r4,r1,0x14`; `bl 0x80103D90`; `b` epilogue; non-negative arm:
///   `lwz r4,4(r31)`; `addi r3,r1,8`; `bl 0x80106B10`; `mr r3,r31`;
///   `addi r4,r1,8`; `bl 0x80103D90`; epilogue; `blr`.
/// - Polarity re-checked: `bc 4,0` is `bf cr0.lt`, so **non-negative** goes to
///   the direct-index arm and a negative index falls into the `count - 1` arm.
///
/// # Arguments
/// - `this`: object whose `+4` word points at the record table.
/// - `index`: signed record index; negative selects the last record.
///
/// # Returns
/// Nothing.
///
/// # Side effects
/// Writes the three words at `this + 0x1C..0x24`.
///
/// # Safety
/// The table and the selected record must be readable and `this + 0x1C..0x24`
/// writable.
///
/// # Confidence
/// high - the branch polarity, the two index forms and both calls are literal.
pub unsafe fn fn_80103db0(this: usize, index: i32) {
    let mut triple = crate::platform::GuestLocal::new( [0u32; 3]);
    let base = platform::read_u32(this + 4) as usize;
    let selected = if index >= 0 {
        index
    } else {
        platform::read_u32(base + 8) as i32 - 1
    };
    wm_transform_record_copy(triple.as_mut_ptr() as usize, base, selected);
    fn_80103d90(this, triple.as_mut_ptr() as usize);
}

