// `if ((unaff_GQR0 & 0x3f00) != 0) { ldexpf(...); }`. `GQR0` is cleared by
// `0x801AB170..0x801AB190` and restored only from the saved thread context at
// `0x802EA6FC`, and no instruction ever loads a non-zero value into it, so the
// `ldexpf` result is discarded and the plain float operands are the body.
//// `GQR3`: `0x8025EEB0`, `0x802AB4F0` and `0x802C2750` all write `0x00050005`// to `GQR3` (`li r3,5` / `oris r3,r3,5`), i.e. type `5` (unsigned 16-bit) with
// scale `0`. `0x801AB174` only clears the GQRs at boot. The three
// `psq_l f1,0(r5),1,3` idioms in `0x8011D930`/`0x8011DA30`/`0x8011DAB0`// therefore load the stored halfword as an unsigned 16-bit value with no
// scaling, which is what the bodies below use.
